nearly all the skills that you'll need  to get ahead in a valent can be  sharpened before you even play the game  Valeria is an experience that combines  elements from many other shooters to  create something that puts a new twist  on familiar ideas fortunately by playing  the games it's inspired by you can get  ahead of the curve and sharpen your  skills before you're even recruited as  an agent valor --nt Counter Strike  global Offensive is the game that valor  --nt has the most similarities with from  the bomb planting and defusing format  and round by round weapon economy to  spray patterns and recoil control so it  makes sense that csgo would be the  perfect training ground and since it's  free-to-play these days you've got no  excuse not to use it as a virtual  stepping-stone to valor and success the  first thing I'll touch on is how you  navigate the maps as the movement that  both csgo and valor and have used some  classic mobility options that those that  haven't checked out CS may not be  familiar with the absence of a sprint ki  will be immediately noticeable to many  players out there with the speed of your  locomotion limited by whatever gun  you're holding the heavier the gun the  slower the run and in the wise words of  fps Doug you run faster with a knife  while it may be unfamiliar to sum the  movement fits the style of the game  perfectly as each move must be  considered whether you're approaching a  bomb site silently with the use of your  walkie and covering your angles or  rushing in an attempt to get an entry  frag and lock the location down how you  navigate the map is drilled into you  quickly while playing counter-strike  and will be a valuable transferable  skill when you come to play valor ins as  you go from your spawns your bomb site  you need to keep in mind all angles  you're approaching from or defending  most of this is going to come with  experience and map knowledge but games  like csgo or Rainbow six siege can teach  you about holding angles or safely  navigating avenues of fire for example  if you're on the defending side you  obviously need to know where the  attackers are coming from but also the  most advantageous angles as well as  develop the patience of holding the  angle so you can prevent their approach  similarly when it comes to attacking a  little experience on how to enter a site  goes a long way approaching from cover  and slowly peeking around an angle with  your crosshair trained on possible enemy  locations as you check inch by inch  until you get a franc or can move up  safely is a technique known as slicing  the pie and can  you can die ever so quickly in these  games it's imperative to give yourself  every advantage but while it's all well  and good learning how to get around the  map or approach an objective it's not  going to be much good if you don't know  how to hold your own against the enemy  team shooting in both CS and Vallance is  also quite different to games you may  already be experienced with the first  thing to keep in mind is that you should  not be shooting and moving movement adds  an extreme penalty to your accuracy in  both these games to the point that  running towards an objective with the  left mouse button held down is about as  useful as a chocolate teapot if you're  using a default crosshair then you'll  see it change and expand when you're  suffering a movement penalty in both  counter-strike and valor in the first  shot you fire from a rifle is nearly  always dead-on accurate if you are  stationary so your best bet when it  comes to winning one-on-one engagements  is to stop crouched aim and fire keeping  all this in mind takes some practice  though obviously another huge factor is  how hot your aim is how fast you can get  your crosshair onto a target  and your recoil control is going to be a  huge help to and there are tons of  training environments to download for  csgo via the Steam marketplace that  allow you to practice recoil tap  shooting headshots and target  acquisition these are the perfect places  for you to learn how to control each  weapon and their recoil when you fire  your weapons full auto you'll notice  there is quite a significant climb to  them after the first couple of shots  more so than most FPS games out there to  really make the most of these weapons  you're going to have to learn how to  bring those rounds back on targets using  these training arenas can give you a  visualization of where your bullets are  going by after toying around with them  in both counter-strike and valent for a  little while I noticed that some of the  main weapons have extremely similar  spray patterns so loading in one game  should help in huge ways when it comes  to hop to the next just so you can see  for yourself here are the recoil  patterns side by side for csgo is ak-47  and valor insuk Waveland weapon the  Vandal and again for the m4a1-s and the  phantom suppressed rifle now are they  exactly the same no are they similar  enough that learning how to control a  weapon in counter-strike will help you  in valor an absolutely the difference  with valor --nt is the  aiming-down-sights where the weapon will  reduce the spread you experience at the  cost of rate of fire although personally  I found it much easier to stick to the  classic way of aiming  the one thing that's fairly unique to  valor when it comes to combat however is  the weight abilities play into  engagements and while Counter Strike or  Rainbow six aren't going to allow you to  practice with special agent talents  there are some fundamentals that can  still be learned for example learning  how smokes can help obscure a line of  sight when attacking or prevent a push  when you're defending will serve you  well once you finally get your hands on  some of the valor and agent abilities  learning how to use a flashbang in csgo  or Rainbow six will also make you feel  more comfortable when it comes to using  Phoenix's curveballs  but what's available to you during each  round of combat depends on your economy  this may be the element of the game  that's most alien to players new to this  style of FPS but it plays an enormous  part of the dynamic the money you have  available to buy your weapons and  abilities at the start of each round  will depend on your performance both at  a team level and an individual level  there may be moments where you have to  buy a weapon for a broke teammate or if  you're on a losing streak do what's  called an eco round this isn't the fight  it's stealing lunch money  ballerina features a UI to help teams  coordinate together when it comes to  working around their economy but a few  hours of csgo will also drive home how  important is element of the game is and  how you can take advantage of it sure  you may have gotten all those kills last  round and now have a wallet brimming  with cash but these games are very much  a team effort so share the wealth a  little and be sure you're picking up the  fancy expensive stuff dropped by other  players at the end of each skirmish this  will no doubt help you save a few more  bucks for a rainy day and finally  remember Intel is the most valuable  thing of all in these games whether  we're talking about Valerie rainbow  succeed your counter-strike don't be  afraid to communicate with your team and  keep everyone informed getting the first  kill of the game can change the dynamic  and create an opening that you need to  tell your squad about spotting a bomb or  spike as is known in valent while  defending a site likely means or  approaching with the objective to plant  so tell your team you've found the  device so you can secure it or even  something as simple as letting your  comrades know that a certain player  favors a sniping position may allow them  to be better informed and obstruct their  vision but these and many lessons more  are best learn by playing and jumping  into csgo or siege will certainly help  you prepare for the combat to come many  times I've spoken to some of the top  performing allied agents I've been  matched with and I've asked have you  played this game much  Mikami responses no but I've played a  lot of counter-strike over the years so  if you want to get a little more  hands-on learning before you get access  to the game these are the titles will  allow you to get your action-packed  studying on before you play valent if  you think this might be of help to you  or your teammates be sure to LIKE the  video share it among your squad and if  you're hungry for more valor and then  you can check out our impressions piece  by clicking that spot in the top right  but to keep looped in for future value  and content then make sure you subscribe  to GameSpot  [Music]

